{
  "id": 2102451,
  "name": "Purple Aether",
  "description": "<p><span style=\"color:rgb(121, 80, 242)\"><strong>Purple Aether Lora Overview:</strong></span></p><ul><li><p><span style=\"color:rgb(121, 80, 242)\"><strong>Weight Range:</strong> 0.7 - 1</span></p></li><li><p><span style=\"color:rgb(121, 80, 242)\"><strong>Trigger words:</strong> Purpl3A3th3r</span></p></li></ul><p><strong>Description:</strong></p><p>Purple Aether was designed to replicate dramatic gel lighting filter photography and more dramatic, stylized poses for SDXL models. The focus was on creating high-contrast, vibrant imagery with dynamic lighting and energy. By experimenting with color combinations and lighting effects, the model produces striking, visually intense scenes that emphasize bold contrasts and dramatic flair. This Lora is something I\u2019ve been experimenting with, and while it's totally possible to achieve these results manually, it just makes it a lot easier and faster to do so with a Lora.</p><p>It's important to note that some of the images generated using Purple Aether do have a tiny bit of post-processing, such as adjustments to saturation levels, shadows/highlights, and the addition of film grain. I find it more reliable to do this kind of fine-tuning in post-processing (using ComfyUI nodes) rather than baking it all in from the start.</p><p><strong>Compatibility:</strong></p><p>I\u2019ve tested Purple Aether on models several SDXL I prefer using them with Epicrealism XL and Lustify but i am sure it should work with others as well, and it works especially well with vibrant, high-contrast styles. But don't hesitate to experiment with other combinations to see what results you get!</p><hr /><p>  Let me know if you like it or hate it (noone can hate this... right?! )</p>",
